.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt DRM-KMS 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm drm-kms ,
.Nm kms
.Nd kernel mode setting on a DRM card
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A card node of
.Xr drm 4
whose driver sets modes describes its display as mode objects and changes
it by commits, as Linux 6.12 does.
The structures and numbers are those of
.In drm/drm_mode.h
and
.In drm/drm_fourcc.h .
.Pp
A CRTC scans out a mode and composes planes: one primary plane, at most
one cursor plane, and any overlay planes.
An encoder feeds a connector from a CRTC it can take, and a connector is an
output a monitor is plugged into, with the modes of its EDID.
A framebuffer is a buffer object, or up to four, in a fourcc format with a
modifier, and a blob is bytes a property names, such as a mode or an EDID.
Each object has an ID, the lowest free from 1, and properties: each a
name, flags and values, a range, an enum, a bitmask, a blob or an object.
.Pp
The client capabilities of
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_SET_CLIENT_CAP
change what a file sees.
.Dv DRM_CLIENT_CAP_UNIVERSAL_PLANES
lists the primary and cursor planes beside the overlays;
.Dv DRM_CLIENT_CAP_ATOMIC
sets that one too, shows the properties marked
.Dv DRM_MODE_PROP_ATOMIC
and allows
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_ATOMIC ;
.Dv DRM_CLIENT_CAP_ASPECT_RATIO
gives the CEA modes their picture aspect in their flags.
.Ss Reading the display
.Bl -tag -width DRM_IOCTL_MODE_OBJ_GETPROPERTIES
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_GETRESOURCES
The IDs of the framebuffers the file made, the CRTCs, the connectors and
the encoders, and the limits of a framebuffer's size.
An array is filled when its count is at least the number there is; each
count is set to that number.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_GETPLANERESOURCES
The planes the file's capabilities list.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_GETPLANE
A plane's CRTC and framebuffer, the CRTCs it can take as a mask by index,
and its formats.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_GETCRTC
A CRTC's mode, the primary plane's framebuffer and place, and its gamma
size.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_GETENCODER
An encoder's type, its CRTC and the CRTCs it can take.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_GETCONNECTOR
A connector's type, state, size in millimetres, encoders, properties and
modes.
A call by the master with
.Fa count_modes
0 probes the monitor first: its EDID becomes the connector's
.Ql EDID
blob, cleaned as Linux cleans it, and its modes are read in Linux's order,
checked against the driver's limits, and sorted preferred first, then by
size, refresh and clock.
A connected monitor without a usable EDID has the DMT modes up to
1024x768 at 60 Hz, and a DisplayPort one 640x480 preferred.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_OBJ_GETPROPERTIES
An object's property IDs and values.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_GETPROPERTY
A property's name, flags, range or enum names.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_GETPROPBLOB
A blob's length, and its bytes when the buffer holds them.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_GETGAMMA
A CRTC's gamma table, whose size the caller gives.
.El
.Ss Blobs and framebuffers
.Bl -tag -width DRM_IOCTL_MODE_OBJ_GETPROPERTIES
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CREATEPROPBLOB
A blob of the caller's bytes, which the file owns.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_DESTROYPROPBLOB
Let go of a blob the file owns; a property that names it keeps it.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_ADDFB2
A framebuffer of a format, with a handle, pitch and offset for each of the
format's planes, and with
.Dv DRM_MODE_FB_MODIFIERS
a modifier.
A plane must take the format and modifier, and each object must hold its
part.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_ADDFB
A framebuffer of one object in the format Linux names for a bpp and
depth.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_GETFB , Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_GETFB2
A framebuffer's size, format, pitches, offsets and modifier, with a handle
to each object for the master or root, and 0 for any other caller.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_RMFB
Remove a framebuffer the file made, from every plane that shows it first,
turning off a CRTC whose primary plane it is when the driver needs that.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CLOSEFB
Let go of a framebuffer the file made and leave it on the screen.
.El
.Pp
A file's close removes each framebuffer it made as
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_RMFB
does and lets go of its blobs.
Each plane's
.Ql IN_FORMATS
blob is a
.Vt struct drm_format_modifier_blob :
its formats, and for each modifier the formats it goes with.
.Ss Atomic commits
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_ATOMIC
sets properties of CRTCs, planes and connectors in one state, checks it
whole, and makes it the display's, or nothing.
Its flags are
.Bl -tag -width DRM_MODE_ATOMIC_ALLOW_MODESET
.It Dv DRM_MODE_ATOMIC_TEST_ONLY
Check the state and change nothing.
.It Dv DRM_MODE_ATOMIC_ALLOW_MODESET
Allow a change of a mode, of a CRTC's active state or of a connector's
CRTC; without it such a commit is
.Er EINVAL .
.It Dv DRM_MODE_ATOMIC_NONBLOCK
Return before the new state reaches the screen.
A commit whose in-fences have not signalled is finished by the kernel's
.Ql kdrm
thread once they have.
.It Dv DRM_MODE_PAGE_FLIP_EVENT
Queue a
.Dv DRM_EVENT_FLIP_COMPLETE
for each CRTC of the state, with the call's
.Fa user_data ,
sent at the vblank that shows the state.
.It Dv DRM_MODE_PAGE_FLIP_ASYNC
Flip without waiting for the vblank, on a driver that can.
.El
.Pp
A plane's
.Ql FB_ID
and
.Ql CRTC_ID
show a framebuffer on a CRTC,
.Ql SRC_X ,
.Ql SRC_Y ,
.Ql SRC_W
and
.Ql SRC_H
choose its part in 16.16 fixed point, and
.Ql CRTC_X ,
.Ql CRTC_Y ,
.Ql CRTC_W
and
.Ql CRTC_H
its place in pixels.
.Ql IN_FENCE_FD
is a sync file whose fence the commit waits for before the plane is
programmed.
A CRTC's
.Ql ACTIVE
turns it on,
.Ql MODE_ID
is a blob of a
.Vt struct drm_mode_modeinfo ,
.Ql GAMMA_LUT ,
.Ql DEGAMMA_LUT
and
.Ql CTM
are colour blobs, and
.Ql OUT_FENCE_PTR
is where to write the descriptor of a sync file whose fence signals at the
vblank that shows the state.
A connector's
.Ql CRTC_ID
routes it.
.Pp
A commit that leaves a CRTC on or turns it on or off ends at the vblank
that shows its state: it is pending until then, and its events are sent and
its out-fence signals then.
A blocking commit waits for the flip it makes, for a flip pending on any
CRTC it changes, and for a nonblocking commit still queued on its in-fences
that changes any CRTC, plane or connector it changes; a
.Dv DRM_MODE_ATOMIC_NONBLOCK
commit in either case is
.Er EBUSY .
The descriptor of each out-fence is reserved and written before the commit
takes effect, so that a descriptor that cannot be written fails the commit;
the sync file is given to the caller once the commit has succeeded, and a
commit that fails writes \-1 to each.
.Ss Vblanks and events
A CRTC whose driver takes its vblank interrupt counts the vblanks it scans
out, in 64 bits, and stamps each with the
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
time.
The interrupt is on while a wait or a queued event holds it, and goes off at
the first vblank five seconds after the last holder lets go, as Linux's
default
.Ql vblankoffdelay
does; the count does not move while it is off.
A CRTC that comes on adds one to its count.
.Pp
Each call takes a CRTC that is on, and is
.Er EINVAL
for one that is off.
A count requested in 32 bits is taken as the 64-bit count nearest the
current one, and a count at most 2^23 behind the current one has passed.
.Bl -tag -width DRM_IOCTL_CRTC_QUEUE_SEQUENCE
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_WAIT_VBLANK
Wait on the CRTC whose index the bits of
.Dv _DRM_VBLANK_HIGH_CRTC_MASK
give, or 1 with
.Dv _DRM_VBLANK_SECONDARY ,
or 0, for a count:
.Dv _DRM_VBLANK_ABSOLUTE
or
.Dv _DRM_VBLANK_RELATIVE
to the current one, and with
.Dv _DRM_VBLANK_NEXTONMISS
the next vblank when that count has passed.
The reply holds the count reached and its time.
A wait ends at the latest after 3 seconds, with
.Er EBUSY .
With
.Dv _DRM_VBLANK_EVENT
the call returns at once and queues a
.Dv DRM_EVENT_VBLANK
with
.Fa request.signal
as its user data; the reply holds the count it waits for.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_CRTC_GET_SEQUENCE
Whether a CRTC is enabled, its count, and the time of its last vblank in
nanoseconds.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_CRTC_QUEUE_SEQUENCE
Queue a
.Dv DRM_EVENT_CRTC_SEQUENCE
for a count, absolute or with
.Dv DRM_CRTC_SEQUENCE_RELATIVE
relative to the current one, the next with
.Dv DRM_CRTC_SEQUENCE_NEXT_ON_MISS
when it has passed;
.Fa sequence
is set to the count it waits for.
.El
.Pp
An event is sent at the vblank its count names, or at once when that count
has passed, and events are read from the card's descriptor in the order
they are sent, as
.Xr drm 4
says: by count, and at one count in the order they were queued.
A
.Dv DRM_EVENT_VBLANK
or
.Dv DRM_EVENT_FLIP_COMPLETE
holds the count in 32 bits, the time in seconds and microseconds and the
CRTC's ID; a
.Dv DRM_EVENT_CRTC_SEQUENCE
holds the count in 64 bits and the time in nanoseconds.
A CRTC that goes off sends every event it has queued at once, with the
count it reached.
On a driver without vblank interrupts the vblank calls are
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ,
and a commit's events are sent as it ends, with count 0.
.Ss The calls before atomic
Each is made one commit, as Linux's helpers make it.
.Bl -tag -width DRM_IOCTL_MODE_OBJ_SETPROPERTY
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_SETCRTC
Set a CRTC's mode, framebuffer, offset and connectors, or turn it off;
framebuffer \-1 keeps the one shown.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_SETPLANE
Show a framebuffer on a plane, or hide the plane for framebuffer 0.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CURSOR , Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CURSOR2
Set the cursor from an ARGB8888 buffer object, hide it for handle 0, or
move it, through the CRTC's cursor plane;
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CURSOR2
gives the hotspot as well.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_PAGE_FLIP
Show another framebuffer of the same format on a CRTC's primary plane,
without a modeset, with an event when asked;
.Er EINVAL
while DPMS leaves the CRTC off.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_DIRTYFB
Give the planes that show a framebuffer its changed rectangles as
.Ql FB_DAMAGE_CLIPS ,
on a driver that flushes them.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_SETGAMMA
Load a CRTC's gamma table and commit it as
.Ql GAMMA_LUT ,
or
.Ql DEGAMMA_LUT
on a CRTC with only that.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_OBJ_SETPROPERTY , Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_SETPROPERTY
Set one property; a connector's
.Ql DPMS
turns its CRTC off, or on while any of its connectors is on.
.El
.Ss Errors
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Er EACCES
A plane's or connector's
.Ql CRTC_ID
of no CRTC.
.It Er EBUSY
A page flip of a CRTC whose primary plane shows nothing; a
.Dv DRM_MODE_ATOMIC_NONBLOCK
commit or page flip on a CRTC whose flip is pending, or that changes a
CRTC, plane or connector of a nonblocking commit still queued on its
in-fences; a
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_WAIT_VBLANK
that waited 3 seconds.
.It Er EFAULT
An array or a value out of the caller's memory.
.It Er EINVAL
An unknown flag; a value a property does not allow; a modeset without
.Dv DRM_MODE_ATOMIC_ALLOW_MODESET ;
an event without a CRTC;
a format, modifier, pitch or size the device does not take; a framebuffer
a plane cannot show; a route an encoder cannot take; a page flip that
changes the format or names a target vblank; a blob of length 0 or of no
ID; a vblank call on a CRTC that is off, of an index the device does not
have, with
.Dv _DRM_VBLANK_SIGNAL
or with an unknown flag; or
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_ATOMIC
by a file without the atomic capability.
.It Er EINTR
A signal came during a blocking commit's wait for a pending flip or for its
in-fences, or during
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_WAIT_VBLANK .
.It Er ENOENT
An ID of no object of the kind asked, a property an atomic commit names
that the object does not have, or a framebuffer of another file.
.It Er ENOMEM
No room for another event in the file's 4096 bytes.
.It Er ENOSPC
A plane's source outside its framebuffer.
.It Er ENOSYS
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_SETGAMMA
on a CRTC without a gamma table, or
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_DIRTYFB
on a driver that needs no flush.
.It Er ENXIO
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CURSOR
on a CRTC without a cursor.
.It Er EOPNOTSUPP
A device without mode setting, an atomic call on a driver without atomic
commits, a vblank call on a driver without vblank interrupts, or a lease
call.
.It Er EPERM
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_DESTROYPROPBLOB
of a blob another file owns.
.It Er ERANGE
A size or place past 32 bits, or a
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_SETCRTC
offset past 16 bits.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr drm 4 ,
.Xr dma-buf 7
.Sh STANDARDS
The calls, structures, properties, errors, mode lists and the order of
object IDs are Linux 6.12's.
.Sh BUGS
A target vblank of a page flip is
.Er EINVAL ,
as Linux answers for a driver without one.
The lease calls are
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
The EDID's quirks, DisplayID blocks, stereo modes and tiles are not read.
